  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $Y\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 12.18) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{12.18-0}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 2.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/c7af386057d2fba19697c0ad87c93a1865c69efaa1a5134f50bff6c7b9143dbe.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\u03a6(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.03) & =1-P(Z<2.03) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 2.03) \\\\  & =1-\u03a6(2.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\u03a6(2.03)=0.9788.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.03) & =1-\u03a6(2.03) \\\\  & =1-0.9788 \\\\  & =0.0212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 12.18)=0.0212.\n$$",
